            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

During the blower door test, we identified the following air leakage (heat loss) locations:

** Around electrical outlets on exterior walls. I recommend to outfit electrical outlets located along the exterior walls with an electrical wall plate gasket

** Both front and back doors. I recommend replacing the weather stripping along all of your exterior doors. Using a screw in aluminum style weather stripping may help to create a better seal, as well as using an adjustable strike plate to ensure the door makes good contact with the weather stripping while closed. 

** The ductwork for the furnace is allowing air infiltration (as well as around the vents themselves), which indicates the significant air infiltration coming up from the crawlspace/floors.  Upon observing the crawlspace, the floor above has quite a few openings and is not well sealed. A continuous air barrier should be implemented throughout the floor, which will help reduce the air infiltration and make the floors feel much warmer during winter. 

** Around the window frames. For any windows that are not being replaced, I recommend removing the trim and examining how the windows were sealed. Commonly, fiberglass batt insulation was used (stuffed into the gap), however this method is not an ideal air barrier. I recommend removing and applying low expansion sprayfoam around the windows. 

Savings: I modeled a modest 10% reduction in air leakage, that is estimated to save $ 50 per year in energy costs and decrease your annual energy usage by 2.0 GJ, and qualify for $ 500 in rebates from the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP). Achieving a minimum 20% reduction qualifies for $750, and a minimum 30% reduction qualifies for $1,000.

Unless you are participating in the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP), that requires a minimum additional R10 insulation be added to the above foundation, main floor exterior walls, there are currently no rebates for air leakage reduction.

NOTE: Please see the "Additional Comments" section of this report for important information about the DHERP, and your eligibility to participate.

</airSeal>
                <mainWalls>

The above foundation exterior walls of your house appear to be 2x4 with R14 batt insulation, as well as 1.5 XPS on the exterior. I recommend you add a minimum R10 insulation to these exterior walls, in conjunction with a future planned upgrade to your exterior siding.

Under the new Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program that was launched on April 1, 2019, there are 3 tiers of rebates associated with how much insulation is added ($3,000 for adding R10 - R14, $5,000 for adding R15 - R19, and $8,000 for adding R20+).

Savings:

By increasing 100% of the exterior walls by R10, you could potentially save $ 170 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 6.1 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 3,000 in rebates from the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP). 

NOTE: Please see the "Additional Comments" section of this report for important information about the DHERP, and your eligibility to participate.

</mainWalls>
                <foundation>

The floor above your crawl space appears to be 2x12 joists with only R12 insulation. I have modelled bringing the insulation up to R40 between the 2x12 joists. This would also be the ideal time to install a continuous barrier for the air leakage mentioned above. 



Savings: By increasing the insulation to R40 between the joists of the floor above your crawlspace, you could potentially save $ 140 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 6.0 GJ, and qualify for up to a $ 2,000 rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP) if you complete an eligible above foundation wall insulation upgrade.

NOTE: Please see the "Additional Comments" section of this report for important information about the DHERP, and your eligibility to participate.

If you do not to complete a DHERP eligible above foundation wall insulation upgrade, you would still be eligible for up to a $ 1,000 from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors.

For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

</foundation>
                <windows>

I have modeled the energy savings associated with upgrading all of the windows (5 total) with ENERGY STAR?????? qualified windows rated for climate zone 3. In addition, I recommend full frame replacement windows that are triple pane, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged windows, with a U-Value (rate of heat loss) no higher than 0.21 BTU per hour.

Savings: By replacing these windows with ones that meet the criteria listed above, you could potentially save $ 230 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 9.4 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 1,250 in rebates (5 existing windows @ $250 each) through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP) if you complete an eligible above foundation wall insulation upgrade.

Unless you are participating in the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP), that requires a minimum additional R10 insulation be added to the above foundation, main floor exterior walls, there are currently no rebates for window upgrades.

NOTE: Please see the "Additional Comments" section of this report for important information about the DHERP, and your eligibility to participate.

</windows>
                <HeatingSystem>

Your house is currently heated with an 83% efficient oil furnace. In conjunction with a Deep Home Energy Retrofit of your house (adding more insulation and reducing the air leakage) I have modeled an upgrade to an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified high-efficiency condensing propane furnace with a minimum efficiency of 95% AFUE, including a high efficiency ECM motor to circulate the heat.

Savings: By replacing your current oil furnace with a 95% AFUE condensing propane furnace, that incorporates an ECM fan blower, you could potentially save $ 340 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 12.4 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 3,000 from the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP). 

NOTE: Please see the "Additional Comments" section of this report for important information about the DHERP, and your eligibility to participate.

If you do not to complete a DHERP eligible wall insulation upgrade, you would still be eligible for up to $ 1,500 from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors.

For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

</HeatingSystem>
                <AdditionalComments>

Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP) rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total above foundation, exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits, and excludes any new additions. To be eligible for a rebate through the DHERP, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the Arctic Energy Alliance.



DHERP rebates are provided on a first-come, first-serve basis for as long as funding remains available. Receiving a pre-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation does not in itself guarantee the availability of funding. Homeowners who have demonstrated their intention to proceed with an eligible above foundation, exterior wall insulation upgrade will be:

      A. Provided with a conditional "Pre-Authorization" of DHERP funding, if funding is still available; or

      B. Placed on a DHERP funding waiting list, should funding become available



For more information and program guidelines, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program



As you research if you will indeed participate in the DHERP, please note the upgrade recommendations outlined within this report are estimated to qualify for up to $11,750 in rebates. The following are the estimated rebates per component of the DHERP:



     - $3,000 for the wall insulation

     - $1,250 for the windows

     - $2,000 for the foundation insulation

     - $3,000 for the heating system

     - $500 for the air leakage reduction

     - $2,000 for the bonus rebate



NOTE: If you do not proceed with an eligible main floor wall insulation upgrade, than you are not eligible for the DHERP - and the only rebates from the list above that would still apply would be the attic &amp; foundation insulation and the heating system, however these would only be 50% of what is listed above.



In addition to the estimated DHERP rebates outlined above, you are also eligible for other rebates from Energy Efficiency Rebate Program (http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program), including the following items that may be of interest to you for this house:

     - ECM domestic water circulation pump - 50% up to $175

     - Programmable Thermostat - 25% up to $175

     - LED Lighting - see section further down



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



LED Lights ????????? Hydro Community

Savings: Light Emitting Diode (LED) lights save about 80% compared to regular incandescent lighting, and about 20% compared to fluorescent lighting. Fluorescent lighting also contains mercury which is a hazardous waste material that requires special disposal. And LED lights work well in the cold and can last a long time.

Rebates: In Yellowknife, LED light bulbs, tubes and integrated fixtures are eligible for a rebate of 50% of the purchase price up to $ 200 (per fiscal year) from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 

To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 

For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Ventilation

Your home currently has the following mechanical ventilation that vents to the outside of the house:  

        - one bathroom fan

        - a clothes dryer



You may want to consider installing ENERGY STAR rated bathroom fans that seal well, use little electricity &amp; operate at low noise levels. Some newer bathroom fans have a built-in humidity sensor. Many homes now have timer switches connected to bathroom fans (where you select 5, 10, 15, 20 minutes) so these fans can remain on for an adequate time to remove bathroom moisture and/or odors without the risk of being left on all day and wasting heat.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Fuel Costs - Yellowknife

The following Dec. 1, 2020 values, including GST, have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Yellowknife:

Electricity - $19.04 min, $0.33 per kWh

Oil  - $0.91 per litre 

Propane - $0.61

Wood - $420.00 per cord delivered; $100 per cord self-harvested

Pellets - $5.67 per bag, $353.85 per tonne bulk delivered



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Disclaimer

The foregoing report is provided as an assessment of the visible components only and shall not be taken as a guarantee or an acceptance of liability for future performance of any part of the building. This report is prepared with reasonable skill and care within the limitations of a visual inspection on the inspection date. This evaluation and report do not cover code compliance issues set by the authority having jurisdiction.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Your Energy Advisor



Please do not hesitate to contact me if you have any questions.



Sincerely,



Michael Goodwin, Senior Energy Management Specialist

Arctic Energy Alliance

Phone: (867) 920-3333

Fax: (867) 873-0303

E-mail: michael.goodwin@aea.nt.ca

Arctic Energy Alliance: Taking action on high heating and power bills, high transportation costs, and climate change!

</AdditionalComments>
